WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.100
How do Turing machines perform

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.580
arithmetic and manage lists? The

3
00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.660
tape model can implement nested

4
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.640
loops and repeated comparisons using

5
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.680
only finite control plus marks.

6
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.060
We will verify multiplication of

7
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.120
block lengths, then detect duplicate

8
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.580
entries in a delimiter-separated binary

9
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.080
list.

10
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.060
For a to the i,

11
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.700
b to the j, c

12
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:22.700
to the k with i

13
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.200
times j equal to k,

14
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.080
first check the input has

15
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.980
nonempty ordered a, b and

16
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.780
c blocks. Choose one unprocessed

17
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.360
a, then scan the b

18
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.100
block. For each b in

19
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.200
that pass, mark one previously

20
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.720
unmarked c, rejecting if no

21
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:39.580
such c exists.

22
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.180
At the end of the

23
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.360
b pass, restore its temporary

24
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.960
marks so the next a

25
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.260
can reuse every b. Keep

26
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.200
the selected a permanently processed

27
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.860
and keep the c symbols

28
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.480
already consumed marked. Mixing up

29
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.660
permanent and temporary marks would

30
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:57.480
change the multiplication into a

31
00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.160
different count.

32
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.200
On the lecture example a

33
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.240
a b c c, there

34
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.740
are two a symbols and

35
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.360
one b. The first outer

36
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.820
round uses that b to

37
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.880
consume one c, and after

38
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.440
restoring b the second outer

39
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.040
round consumes the other c.

40
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.340
No unprocessed a or c

41
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.500
remains, so the example accepts

42
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.360
because two times one equals

43
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:19.760
two.

44
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.900
After every symbol in the

45
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.320
first block has been processed,

46
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.520
reject if any unmarked c

47
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.180
remains. Also reject immediately if

48
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.260
the c block runs out

49
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.320
during a pass through the

50
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.880
b block. The outer loop

51
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.660
processes a fresh symbol each

52
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.540
round, and each round consumes

53
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.580
exactly j copies of c,

54
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.700
proving both termination and the

55
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:40.800
multiplication condition.

56
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.560
For element distinctness, the input

57
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.800
is a list of binary

58
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.120
strings separated by hash markers.

59
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.080
In the lecture example the

60
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.760
first entry is zero then

61
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.640
one, the second is one

62
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.400
then zero, and the third

63
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.300
is zero then one. The

64
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.960
first and third match, but

65
00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.640
the machine must compare each

66
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.320
entry as a whole rather

67
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.880
than just comparing its first

68
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.000
character or length.

69
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.580
Place distinguishable marks on the

70
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.160
delimiters of the current pair

71
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.080
of entries. Zig-zag between corresponding

72
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.020
characters, recording a bit in

73
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.460
finite control, until a mismatch

74
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.820
or both ends are reached.

75
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.380
Restore comparison marks before moving

76
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.880
the second delimiter mark to

77
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.700
the next entry.

78
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.940
Comparing zero one with one

79
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.740
zero finds a mismatch, so

80
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.660
that pair is different. Move

81
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.260
the second-entry selection to the

82
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.940
third entry and compare zero

83
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.000
one with zero one; both

84
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.820
end together with all symbols

85
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.160
matching. That equality proves a

86
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.220
duplicate and the machine rejects

87
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.360
immediately without needing further pair

88
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:41.760
tests.

89
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.960
If no equality is found,

90
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.280
advance the outer delimiter selection

91
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.480
and compare every remaining pair.

92
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.960
With a finite number of

93
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.980
entries and finite per-pair scans,

94
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.200
the algorithm halts. Equal-length but

95
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.320
different entries must pass, while

96
00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.920
repeated empty entries need the

97
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.340
same whole-entry equality logic as

98
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.800
repeated nonempty ones.

99
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.360
Things to take note of.

100
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.060
Distinguish loop counters stored through

101
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.580
marks, temporary comparison marks and

102
00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.180
permanent progress markers. State the

103
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.520
form checks, explain restoration, and

104
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.220
prove that all required products

105
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.700
or entry pairs are covered;

106
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.780
finding one example that works

107
00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:21.320
is not the correctness argument.
